 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322927 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e38f54d9-6db5-595a-a0bd-7f54f38a189a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322948 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322948 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322948 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e61e4c06-d5fa-5f07-8cf1-a2b931de2e10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323336 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323336 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323336 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186321515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323336 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "56691dec-7751-59ca-8f7f-effa3fd14823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323492 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323492 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323492 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323492 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323492 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323492 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323492 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323492 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186322583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323492 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a75f0803-927c-5560-b17b-00d17f941ea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-365878" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323828 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,z,\\,2x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d45c5c95-c352-5bfc-96a4-e8c0f7bd9595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323828 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,z,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,2,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323831 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323831 #edb/ref 1069 "q-365882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323831 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323831 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,x,\\,3x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=-1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323831 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b2de831-186b-5690-a119-3c54805658b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323831 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=-1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 2,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=-1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,x,\\,3x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(3x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-(3x+y))\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(3x+y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(-(3x+y))\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{2}[(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+xy){|}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{2}(6+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[6y+{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362413 #edb/ref 1064 "If we wish to find the flux through a tilted plane, we should first calculate the unit normal vector to this plane.\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle x,\\: 1-x+y,\\: 2y+8\\rangle$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=2x+2y-1$ such that $\\textrm{Area}(S) = 2.$ \nAssume that we need to find the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$\n\nIf $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = -1, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,1-x+y,\\,2y+8⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,1-x+y,\\,2y+8⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2x-2(1-x+y)+2y+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2x-2+2x-2y+2y+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362413 #edb/ref 1065 15974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362413 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "48c45790-f4c7-562f-9a3f-46bf6009dd4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362413 #edb/ref 1067 "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362446 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362446 #edb/ref 1061 657086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362446 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "81d0dc6b-4e76-5f80-934b-e300c50b0201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362446 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369894 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369894 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369894 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369894 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3-x,\\,x+y,\\,z-2x⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=2x+2y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=3.$  Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369894 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e7b422e-c36f-5bad-be46-9f50302437f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369894 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+2y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3-x,\\,x+y,\\,z-2x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨3-x,\\,x+y,\\,2y⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2(3-x)+2(x+y)-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(6-2x+2x+2y-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can 