8 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f3b4139-4781-5e1e-8511-61fe405f4cab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2z+0-3(z-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y-z}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{3y-z}{5}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{S}3y-z\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\boxed{}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5213d5bf-5843-5c8c-8790-014b534c60aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{(-3z)^{2}+(x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{9{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{2}z+2{x}^{2}z+2y(x+y)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,2y,\\,2z-x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\frac{3}{2}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa928e5a-70af-5b00-a980-606761f10bda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=3,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2y,\\,2z-x-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,2y,\\,(2x-y+6)-x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,2y,\\,x-2y+6⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x-2y-2(x-2y+6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}(2y-12)\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-6y]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4c6d18b-d618-53cd-9fce-a599b57a0c5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428947 #edb/ref 1030 0.3333333333333333 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428947 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 3 eligible passed source activities = 0.333333; nearest selected fraction = 1/3. Crossing activity numbers [3]; waits [3]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/3 (1/3). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3178->1930. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428947 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048942 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428948 #edb/ref 1030 0.25 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428948 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 4 eligible passed source activities = 0.250000; nearest selected fraction = 1/4. Crossing activity numbers [3]; waits [3]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/4 (1/4). Selection: most observat